### What is Redis Sentinel?

Sentinel provides high availability for a single-node dataset. One master holds all data; replicas are full copies. Sentinel processes monitor the master via heartbeats and promote a replica by quorum when the master fails. The client asks Sentinels "who is master?" rather than hardcoding an address.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.redis.connection.RedisSentinelConfiguration;
import org.springframework.data.redis.connection.lettuce.LettuceConnectionFactory;

@Configuration
public class SentinelConfig {

    @Bean
    public LettuceConnectionFactory sentinelConnection() {
        RedisSentinelConfiguration config = new RedisSentinelConfiguration()
            .master("mymaster")
            .sentinel("sentinel-1", 26379)
            .sentinel("sentinel-2", 26379)
            .sentinel("sentinel-3", 26379);
        return new LettuceConnectionFactory(config);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
Client connects to Sentinels, asks "who is master?"
Sentinels reply with the current master address.
If master fails, quorum promotes a replica automatically.
```

### What is Redis Cluster?

Cluster shards data across multiple masters. Each key is mapped to a slot by `CRC16(key) % 16384`. Each master owns a range of slots and has replicas for failover.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.redis.connection.RedisClusterConfiguration;
import org.springframework.data.redis.connection.lettuce.LettuceConnectionFactory;
import java.util.List;

@Configuration
public class ClusterConfig {

    @Bean
    public LettuceConnectionFactory clusterConnection() {
        RedisClusterConfiguration config = new RedisClusterConfiguration(
            List.of("node-1:6379", "node-2:6379", "node-3:6379"));
        config.setMaxRedirects(3);
        return new LettuceConnectionFactory(config);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class SlotCalculation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Cluster routes by slot
        String key = "session:SAV2001";
        // int slot = CRC16.hash(key) % 16384; // e.g. slot 8742 --> Master-B
        System.out.println("Key '" + key + "' maps to a slot via CRC16 % 16384");
        System.out.println("Cluster routes the request to the master owning that slot.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Key 'session:SAV2001' maps to a slot via CRC16 % 16384
Cluster routes the request to the master owning that slot.
```

### How do Sentinel and Cluster compare?

| Aspect | Sentinel | Cluster |
|---|---|---|
| Sharding | None (one master) | 16384 hash slots across masters |
| Data size | Must fit one node | Scales horizontally with masters |
| Write scaling | No | Yes |
| Read scaling | Yes (read from replicas) | Yes |
| Failover | Quorum of Sentinels | Per-shard (gossip among masters) |
| Multi-key ops | Always work | Only within same slot (use hash tags) |
| Minimum nodes | 1 master + replicas + 3 Sentinels | 3 masters + 3 replicas = 6 |
| Complexity | Lower | Higher |

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class TopologyDecision {
    public static String decide(long dataGb, long qps) {
        if (dataGb <= 25 && qps <= 100_000) {
            return "SENTINEL: data fits one node, QPS within single-master capacity";
        }
        return "CLUSTER: data or QPS exceeds one node, needs horizontal sharding";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(decide(10, 50_000));   // sessions, OTPs
        System.out.println(decide(500, 500_000)); // market-data cache
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
SENTINEL: data fits one node, QPS within single-master capacity
CLUSTER: data or QPS exceeds one node, needs horizontal sharding
```

### When do you use hash tags in Cluster?

Multi-key operations (MGET, MULTI/EXEC, Lua scripts touching multiple keys) only work when all keys land in the same slot. Hash tags force co-location by hashing only the content inside curly braces.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;

public class HashTagExample {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public HashTagExample(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public void demonstrateHashTags() {
        // WRONG: keys for same account land on different slots
        redis.opsForValue().set("balance:SAV2001", "150000");
        redis.opsForValue().set("limit:SAV2001", "500000");
        // MULTI/EXEC across these keys REJECTED in Cluster (different slots)

        // CORRECT: hash tags force same slot
        redis.opsForValue().set("{SAV2001}:balance", "150000");
        redis.opsForValue().set("{SAV2001}:limit", "500000");
        // Only {SAV2001} is hashed --> same slot --> MULTI/EXEC works
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
Without hash tags: CRC16("balance:SAV2001") and CRC16("limit:SAV2001") produce different slots
With hash tags: CRC16("SAV2001") used for both --> same slot --> multi-key ops work
```

### When should you choose Sentinel?

Choose Sentinel for lightweight data that fits on one node: sessions, OTPs, tokens, rate-limit counters. The total dataset is small (under 25 GB), and the primary need is automatic failover, not horizontal scaling.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class SentinelUseCase {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long users = 10_000_000;
        long sessionSizeKb = 1;
        long totalGb = (users * sessionSizeKb) / (1024 * 1024);
        System.out.println("Session store: " + totalGb + " GB");
        System.out.println("Fits one node --> use Sentinel for HA");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Session store: 9 GB
Fits one node --> use Sentinel for HA
```

### When should you choose Cluster?

Choose Cluster when the data volume or request rate exceeds what one node can handle. Market-data caches, product catalogs with millions of items, and high-QPS read-heavy workloads are typical Cluster use cases.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class ClusterUseCase {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long instruments = 50_000_000;
        long priceSizeKb = 10;
        long totalGb = (instruments * priceSizeKb) / (1024 * 1024);
        long qps = 500_000;
        System.out.println("Market-data cache: " + totalGb + " GB, " + qps + " QPS");
        System.out.println("Exceeds one node --> use Cluster for sharding + HA");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Market-data cache: 476 GB, 500000 QPS
Exceeds one node --> use Cluster for sharding + HA
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using Cluster just for HA when the data fits on one node, or forgetting hash tags in Cluster.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class CommonMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: using Cluster just for HA when data fits one node
        // Sentinel already provides HA with less complexity and no hash-tag constraints
        System.out.println("WRONG: 10 GB sessions on Cluster -- unnecessary complexity");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: 10 GB sessions on Sentinel -- simpler, same HA");

        // WRONG: forgetting hash tags in Cluster
        System.out.println("WRONG: MGET balance:SAV2001, limit:SAV2001 -- different slots, rejected");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: MGET {SAV2001}:balance, {SAV2001}:limit -- same slot, works");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: 10 GB sessions on Cluster -- unnecessary complexity
CORRECT: 10 GB sessions on Sentinel -- simpler, same HA
WRONG: MGET balance:SAV2001, limit:SAV2001 -- different slots, rejected
CORRECT: MGET {SAV2001}:balance, {SAV2001}:limit -- same slot, works
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Sentinel favors availability over consistency (AP in CAP terms); during a network partition it may keep serving stale data.
- Cluster failover is per-shard; other shards keep serving while one shard fails over.
- Managed Redis offerings (AWS ElastiCache, Azure Cache) blur the Sentinel vs Cluster distinction but the same decision logic applies.
- Do not reach for Cluster merely for HA; reach for it when you need horizontal scale.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class SeniorNuances {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Sentinel: AP (availability over consistency during partition)");
        System.out.println("Cluster: per-shard failover, other shards unaffected");
        System.out.println("Decision: data size + QPS determines topology, not HA alone");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Sentinel: AP (availability over consistency during partition)
Cluster: per-shard failover, other shards unaffected
Decision: data size + QPS determines topology, not HA alone
```
